### What is this question really testing?

Influence without authority, data-driven persuasion, and collaboration. The worst answer is "I knew I was right so I pushed until they gave in." The best shows you reduced risk, respected concerns, and let the data convince.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class PersuasionTest {
    enum Method { EVIDENCE, EGO }
    public static String evaluate(Method method) {
        return switch (method) {
            case EVIDENCE -> "Strong: data did the convincing";
            case EGO      -> "Weak: pushed opinion without proof";
        };
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(Method.EVIDENCE));
    }
}
```

### How do you understand the current approach first?

The team usually had a good reason for doing things the current way. Understand and respect that reason before proposing a change.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class UnderstandFirst {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Current approach: process transfers one at a time");
        System.out.println("Why: simple, worked at low volume, easy to debug");
        System.out.println("Respect: it was the right choice at low volume");
        System.out.println("Problem now: volume grew, one-at-a-time is too slow");
    }
}
```

### How do you quantify the problem?

Show real numbers from logs or metrics. "Our end-of-day batch halts completely when it hits one bad record, requiring multiple reruns."

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class QuantifyProblem {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(QuantifyProblem.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int avgReruns = 4;
        int minutesPerRerun = 15;
        int totalMinutesWasted = avgReruns * minutesPerRerun;
        log.info("Current: " + avgReruns + " reruns/night x " + minutesPerRerun + "min = " + totalMinutesWasted + "min wasted");
    }
}
```

```text
Current: 4 reruns/night x 15min = 60min wasted
```

### How do you build a proof of concept?

Run both approaches on the same set of transfers and compare results. Show the comparison honestly.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class ProofOfConcept {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(ProofOfConcept.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("One-at-a-time: stops at first error, hides remaining errors");
        log.info("Batch: settles all valid, reports ALL failures in one pass");
        log.info("Time: one-at-a-time = 60min with reruns, batch = 12min single pass");
        log.info("Errors surfaced: one-at-a-time = 1 per run, batch = all at once");
    }
}
```

### How do you present the comparison fairly?

Acknowledge the downsides of your approach. The batch approach is slightly more complex to debug per-record. Name that trade-off.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class FairComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Batch advantages: faster, surfaces all errors at once");
        System.out.println("Batch downside: slightly harder to debug individual records");
        System.out.println("Mitigation: detailed per-record error report in the batch output");
        System.out.println("I named the downside so the team knew I was not overselling.");
    }
}
```

### How do you make the change reversible?

Proposing a change that can be rolled back easily lowers resistance. "We can try batching for one week. If it is worse, we switch back in minutes."

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class ReversibleChange {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Proposal: try batching for 1 week with a feature flag");
        System.out.println("Rollback: flip the flag and we are back to one-at-a-time in seconds");
        System.out.println("Effect: team agreed because the risk was near zero");
    }
}
```

### How do you let the team own the decision?

Present the data and let the team decide. If you push the decision through, adoption is shallow. If the team chooses based on evidence, adoption is genuine.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class TeamOwnership {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Wrong: 'I decided we are doing batching'");
        System.out.println("Right: 'Here are the numbers. What does the team think?'");
        System.out.println("Outcome: team chose batching unanimously based on the evidence");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "What if the team still disagreed?" (respect the decision, try again with more data), "How did it hold up?" (end-of-day errors now handled in a single pass), and "Would you do it differently?" (I would have built the POC earlier).

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class PersuasionFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Still disagreed? -> Respect decision, revisit with more data later");
        System.out.println("Held up? -> Yes, single-pass batch is now standard");
        System.out.println("Differently? -> Build the POC earlier to shorten the debate");
    }
}
```
